= Kinematics

A point of the top has position $vb(r)(t)$ and velocity $vb(v) = dv(vb(r), t)$;
its acceleration is $vb(a) = dv(vb(r), t, 2)$. In components along the unit
vectors $vu(e)_1, vu(e)_2, vu(e)_3$ the arrow notation $va(omega)$ is used for the
angular velocity, so that
$ vb(v) = va(omega) cprod vb(r), quad vb(v) dprod va(omega) = 0, quad
  abs(vb(v))^2 = omega^2 r^2 - (va(omega) dprod vb(r))^2. $ <eq-v>
The derivative can be written in other styles: horizontal $dv(f, x, style: "horizontal")$,
skewed $dv(f, x, 2, style: "skewed")$, as an operator $dv(, t)$ acting on what follows, or
with a finite difference $dv(x, t, d: Delta)$. A large operator adapts to its argument:
$ dv(vec(x, y, z), t, style: "large") = vec(dot(x), dot(y), dot(z)),
  quad pdv(, x, y, style: "large") (x^2 y + sin(x y)). $

Rotations about the axes are generated by
$ R_z (phi) = rot3zmat(phi), quad R_x (theta) = rot3xmat(theta), \
  R(alpha + beta) = rot2mat(alpha + beta, delim: "["), $ <eq-rot>
and $R_y (psi) = rot3ymat(psi)$ completes the set. From @eq-rot the determinant is
$mdet(cos phi, -sin phi; sin phi, cos phi) = 1$.

= Fields

The potential $U(x, y, z)$ gives the force $vb(F) = -grad U$. For a field $vb(A)$,
$ div vb(A) = pdv(A_x, x) + pdv(A_y, y) + pdv(A_z, z), \
  curl (grad U) = vb(0), quad div (curl vb(A)) = 0, quad laplacian U = div (grad U). $ <eq-field>
Mixed partial derivatives add their orders automatically:
$ pdv(f, x, y), quad pdv(f, x, 2), quad pdv(f, x, y, 2), quad pdv(f, x, y, [1, 2]), \
  pdv(f, x, y, z, [2, n, k+1]), quad pdv(f, x, y, [m, n], total: N), quad
  pdv(u, x_i, x_j, [2, 3]), quad pdv(phi, t, d: delta). $ <eq-pdv>
Differentials come with thin spaces: $integral f dd(x)$, $integral.double g dd(x, y)$,
$integral.triple h dd(x, y, z, 3)$, $dd(x, y, [2, 3])$, $dd(x, y, prod: and)$,
$dd(V, compact: #true)$; a variation is $var(S) = 0$, a difference $difference(E)$.

The Jacobian of the map $(r, phi) |-> (x, y)$ and the Hessian of $U$ are
$ J = jmat(x, y; r, phi) = mat(cos phi, -r sin phi; sin phi, r cos phi), quad
  H = hmat(U; x, y, z, big: #true). $ <eq-jac>
Inline they are cramped: $jmat(f, g; u, v)$ and $hmat(f; x, y, delim: "[")$.

Other matrices that appear below:
$ dmat(I_1, I_2, I_3), quad dmat(1, 2, delim: "[", fill: 0), quad admat(a, b, c), quad
  imat(3), quad imat(2, fill: dot.c), quad zmat(2), \
  xmat(3, 3, #((i, j) => $g^(#(i - 1) #(j - 1))$)), quad grammat(vb(u), vb(v)), quad
  grammat(u, v, w, norm: #true, delim: "|"). $ <eq-mats>

